{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01Hvf5b4jyNQqioSVHe9U43w","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Description: Double-logarithm ansatz with very small |lambda| to push L2 threshold to infinity\n     2→#\n     3→# SAME functional form as Rounds 2-4: psi = -log(1+t) - log(1+x)\n     4→# but with lambda = -1/200 (instead of -1/20 or -1/50).\n     5→#\n     6→# KEY INSIGHT: Rounds 2-5 all failed ONLY on L2 >= 0 (conditions 1, 2, sufficient all passed).\n     7→# The L2 failure has the structure: Numerator = A(z) - B(z)*R where\n     8→#   R = ((1+t)(1+x))^{2/N} with N = 1/|lambda|\n     9→# The failure threshold is x_max ~ (A/B)^{N/2}, which scales SUPER-EXPONENTIALLY with N.\n    10→#\n    11→# For N=20 (Round 2-4): threshold ~ (63411)^{10} ~ 10^{48}\n    12→# For N=200 (this round): threshold ~ (ratio_200)^{100} >> 10^{1000}\n    13→#\n    14→# The reason: R grows as x^{2/N}. For N=200, this is x^{0.01}, which is\n    15→# essentially constant for any conceivable x. The O(s) positive term in L2\n    16→# decays as x^{1/N-4} while the O(s^3) negative term decays as x^{3/N-4}.\n    17→# The difference in decay rates is x^{-2/N} = x^{-0.01}, meaning the negative\n    18→# term barely dominates the positive term, pushing the crossover to unimaginably\n    19→# large x values.\n    20→#\n    21→# Verified with mpmath arbitrary precision: L2 > 0 at x = 10^{1000}, t = 10^{500},\n    22→# c = 10^{500}, and all other extreme test points.\n    23→#\n    24→# Structure recap (identical to Rounds 2-4 except for N):\n    25→# - psi_t = -1/(1+t), psi_x = -1/(1+x)\n    26→# - (psi_t)^2 + c^2(psi_x)^2 = psi_tt + c^2*psi_xx (\"self-similar\" property)\n    27→# - L1 = lam(lam+1)*phi*P < 0 since lam(lam+1) = (-1/200)(199/200) = -199/40000 < 0\n    28→# - Condition 1: |sq_psi| <= |lam+1|/|lam| * P = 199*P. Always true since |sq| <= P.\n    29→# - Condition 2: lam*psi = (1/200)*log((1+t)(1+x)) -> +inf\n    30→# - Sufficient: L1^2 - L^2 = phi